### What is the Target Sum problem?

Given an array of non-negative integers and a target T, assign a + or - sign to each number so that the signed sum equals T. Count the number of valid assignments.

At the National Bank of India, an adjustment clerk has pending amounts that can each be posted as a credit or debit, and needs to count how many labellings produce a required net balance.

```java
package com.bank.reconciliation;

public class TargetSumDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {1, 1, 1, 1, 1};
        int target = 3;
        System.out.println("Ways: " + findTargetSumWays(nums, target));
    }

    static int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) total += n;
        if (total + target < 0 || (total + target) % 2 != 0) return 0;
        int subsetSum = (total + target) / 2;

        int[] dp = new int[subsetSum + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int s = subsetSum; s >= num; s--) {
                dp[s] += dp[s - num];
            }
        }
        return dp[subsetSum];
    }
}
```

```text
Ways: 5
```

### How does the algebraic reduction work?

Split the numbers into a positive group P (plus-signed) and a negative group N (minus-signed). We need P - N = target and we know P + N = total. Adding the two equations: 2P = total + target, so P = (total + target) / 2.

Now the problem is: how many subsets sum to P?

```java
// Given: nums = {1, 1, 1, 1, 1}, target = 3
// total = 5
// P = (5 + 3) / 2 = 4
// Count subsets summing to 4:
// {1,1,1,1} -> 4 ways to pick 4 of the five 1s = C(5,4) = 5
```

### Why do we check feasibility first?

If total + target is odd, P would be fractional, which is impossible for integer sums. If total + target is negative, P would be negative, which no subset of non-negative numbers can reach.

```java
package com.bank.reconciliation;

public class FeasibilityGuards {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Odd: (5 + 2) = 7, odd -> 0 ways
        System.out.println(findTargetSumWays(new int[]{1,1,1,1,1}, 2));

        // Target exceeds total: |target| > total -> 0 ways
        System.out.println(findTargetSumWays(new int[]{1,1,1}, 10));
    }

    static int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) total += n;
        if (total + target < 0 || (total + target) % 2 != 0) return 0;
        int subsetSum = (total + target) / 2;
        int[] dp = new int[subsetSum + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int s = subsetSum; s >= num; s--) dp[s] += dp[s - num];
        }
        return dp[subsetSum];
    }
}
```

